statement:
`num` is the decimal form of a positive integer, written without leading zeros, and `t` is a positive integer.

Return, as a string, the smallest number that meets all three conditions:

- it is greater than or equal to `num` (so it may have more digits than `num`);
- none of its digits is `0`;
- the product of its digits is a multiple of `t`.

Return `"-1"` when no number meets them all.

validator:
function validate([num, t]) {
  if (num.length < 2 || num.length > 200000) return 'num needs 2 to 2 * 10^5 digits.';
  if (!/^[0-9]+$/.test(num)) return 'num may contain only digits.';
  if (num[0] === '0') return 'num must not start with 0.';
  if (t < 1 || t > 1e14) return 't is a whole number from 1 to 10^14.';
  return null;
}

